<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiangxi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiangxi is an inland province of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a>, centred on the basin of the Gan River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Poyang Lake</a>, which drain into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. A rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing region, it became famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingdezhen">Jingdezhen</a>. From 1931 to 1934 its mountainous south was the heart of the Chinese Soviet Republic, from which the Communists set out on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiangxi.jpg" alt="Jiangxi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emitchan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SanQingShan9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jiangxi lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in the north and rises into hillier country in the south and east. Mountains surround it on three sides, and the highest point is Mount Huanggang in the Wuyi Mountains, at 2,157 metres. The Gan River flows through the whole province from south to north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Lake Poyang</a>, the largest freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which empties into the Yangtze. The Gan valley was historically the main north-south transport route of south China, one of the few easy corridors through the rugged south-eastern mountains. It carried trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> and the Yangtze valley and the territory of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, so the region was strategically important throughout much of Chinese history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jiangxi lay outside the sphere of early Chinese civilisation under the Shang, and peoples collectively known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> probably lived there. Its north formed the western frontier of the state of Wu, and after Yue conquered Wu in 473 BCE, Chu took northern Jiangxi. Yuzhang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, established at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, was the first commandery set up there by a Chinese dynasty. It took its name from the original name of the Gan River, and Gan is now the province's abbreviation. In 291 it became its own zhou, Jiangzhou, under the Western Jin, and in 733 the Tang divided the Jiangnan Circuit; the western half, the Jiangnanxi Circuit, gave the province its modern name.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a>, Jiangxi Province was established for the first time, and it took roughly its modern borders. After the fall of the Qing it became one of the earliest Communist bases. The Nanchang Uprising took place there on 1 August 1927. The government of the Chinese Soviet Republic was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruijin">Ruijin</a> in 1931, and in 1935, encircled by Nationalist forces, the Communists broke through and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a>. The brutal campaigns and purges of those years reduced the population by 40 per cent, to 13.8 million in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> is the provincial capital, and Ganzhou and Jiujiang are other major cities. Most of the population is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, and the province is the centre of Gan Chinese, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> also spoken. It has deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, tungsten, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, uranium and lithium. After the opening of the Yuehan Railway in 1936 and of the Zhegan Railway in 1937, Jiujiang's port declined in importance. The province was fully under Communist control from the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiangxi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with the Kuomintang collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in <b>Jiangxi</b> in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the Long March of 1934–35 to northern Shaanxi, where Yan’an became its base, during which Mao Zedong rose to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words were written by Xu Wenjing and its music by Song Yang, who adapted it from &quot;Ballad of Picking Tea&quot;, a folk song of <b>Jiangxi</b> belonging to the wider tradition of tea-picking operas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-sun-in-the-sky">Red Sun in the Sky</a></li><li><blockquote>The government army led by Wang Yangming, grand coordinator of southern <b>Jiangxi</b>, captured Zhu Chenhao, Prince of Ning, ending his rebellion against the Zhengde Emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Wang Yangming, governor of southern <b>Jiangxi</b>, raised an army, occupied Nanchang and in August crushed the rebels, capturing the prince.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Chen Han controlled <b>Jiangxi</b> and Huguang and was at first the strongest of the rebel states against the Yuan Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-han">Chen Han</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Nationalist government turned on its Communist allies in 1927, Communist forces under Mao Zedong and Zhu De built rural bases in the mountains between <b>Jiangxi</b> and Fujian.</blockquote><a href="/state/chinese-soviet-republic">Chinese Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
